    "Question #: 9": {
      "Title": "Exam 300-100 topic 1 question 43 discussion - ExamTopics",
      "Question": {
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "value": "After successfully connecting to a remote CIFS share using smbclient, the command mget MyFiles is issued to retrieve the folder MyFiles, including all of its contents. Instead of transferring the folder, smbclient returns the error:"
          },
          {
            "type": "break"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "value": "NT_STATUS_NO_SUCH_FILE listing \\MyFiles"
          },
          {
            "type": "break"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "value": "What should be done in order to download the contents of the folder?"
          }
        ]
      },
      "Choices": {
        "A": {
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "value": "A. The command recurse should be used to toggle recursion for mget and mput."
            }
          ]
        },
        "B": {
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "value": "B. To provide a target for the file transfer, the local folder MyFiles has to be created in advance."
            }
          ]
        },
        "C": {
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "value": "C. The command rget should be used instead of mget to perform recursive operations."
            }
          ]
        },
        "D": {
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "value": "D. The server has to support recursion for the given share which, on Samba, is enabled by setting recursion = true."
            }
          ]
        },
        "E": {
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "value": "E. smbclient should be used invoked with the \"\"recurse or \"\"r parameter."
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      "Answer": {
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "value": "Suggested Answer: D \ud83d\uddf3\ufe0f"
          }
        ]
      },
      "Explanation": {
        "content": []
      },
      "Vote_count": []
    },
    "Question #: 10": {
      "Title": "Exam 300-100 topic 1 question 44 discussion - ExamTopics",
      "Question": {
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "value": "After adding a remote CIFS share to /etc/fstab, the share is mounted to the correct location in the file system. There, all files belong to the user and group root and are not readable or writable by any other users on the system. What should be done in order to permit distinct local user access to the mounted files?"
          }
        ]
      },
      "Choices": {
        "A": {
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "value": "A. The option root squash = yes should be added to the share in the Samba server's smb.conf to allow full access by the client."
            }
          ]
        },
        "B": {
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "value": "B. The mount.cifs options uid, gid, file_mode and dir_mode should be used to specify the ownership and permissions of the mount."
            }
          ]
        },
        "C": {
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "value": "C. The user should be added to the local user group smbusers, as mount.cifs by default restricts access to all CIFS mounts to member of this group in addition to the root account."
            }
          ]
        },
        "D": {
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "value": "D. The mount.cifs command should always be executed by user who will use the mount afterwards. The user should be granted the required permission in / etc/sudoers."
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      "Answer": {
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "value": "Suggested Answer: C \ud83d\uddf3\ufe0f"
          }
        ]
      },
      "Explanation": {
        "content": []
      },
      "Vote_count": []
    }
